course: english
lesson: 16
title: Reading for pleasure and the reading book at home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Reading for pleasure means reading because you want to, not because somebody told you to. It can be a comic under the covers, a gaming guide, a joke book, a fat fantasy novel or an audiobook on a long car ride. Children who read for fun read more, and the more you read, the more words and ideas you meet.
explain: Every Friday Ollie brings home a school book with a yellow sticker and groans, «It's a baby book, bruv» (bruv is London slang for mate or brother). The sticker is a book band: a colour level that matches early reading books to how well a child can read the words alone. The banded book is for practice; a second book of his own choice, read with a grown-up, is for fun.
explain: Kai picks a library book far too hard, and the librarian says, «Try five fingers.» Open a page in the middle and read it; put up one finger for each word you can't read or don't understand. No fingers or one: easy. Two or three: about right. Four or five: too hard to read alone for now, so share it with a grown-up or save it for later.
explain: Any grown-up can help, even one who doesn't read much. Ollie's dad drives a lorry and thinks he can't help, but listening, asking «What happened? What happens next?» and laughing at the funny bits is the real help. In Newcastle, a mum says her son reads under the covers with a torch, «the little bairn» (bairn means child in the North East of England and in Scotland): a child who reads in secret loves reading.
fact: Reading for pleasure means choosing to read because you enjoy it.
fact: Studies have found that children who read for pleasure tend to know more words and do better at school, though it is hard to measure exactly how much the reading itself causes.
fact: Comics and graphic novels are real reading: you work out the story from the words and the pictures together.
fact: Gaming guides, magazines and fact books are real reading too; interest is what keeps a reader going.
fact: Audiobooks build vocabulary, understanding and a love of stories, but they do not practise reading printed words, so children use both.
fact: Hearing a book read aloud still helps children who can read alone: they meet harder books, new words and how expression sounds.
fact: The five-finger test: on one page, count the words you can't read or don't know; four or five means the book is too hard to read alone for now.
fact: A book band is a colour level used in many UK primary schools to match early reading books to a child's reading.
fact: A reading record is a little book where a grown-up notes the reading done at home; a real note («loved the dragon part») tells the teacher more than «read 3 pages, good».
fact: A free reader chooses any book from the library instead of banded books, and still needs to read a little every day.
fact: Talking about books and swapping them with friends makes reading more fun.
fact: Nobody becomes a great reader overnight; readers grow slowly, a little every day.
fact: Slang: «cringe» means embarrassing; «y'all» (southern US) means you all; «that book ate» (Gen Z slang) means it was excellent.
exercise: What is the yellow sticker on Ollie's reading book? | A book band, a colour level for reading | A warning that the book has torn pages | A sign the book is for a younger class | A prize for finishing his last book early
exercise: Ollie calls the banded book a baby book. What is the wise move? | Read it for practice, and choose a second book for fun | Refuse to read anything at all until the teacher gives him a harder one | Throw away the sticker so nobody can see it | Read only the hardest book in the class library
exercise: Mum writes «Read 3 pages, good» every night. What would tell the teacher more? | A real note, like «loved the dragon part» | A note saying exactly how many minutes it took each night | A longer list of every page number read | Nothing at all, the signature is enough
exercise: Ollie's dad drives a lorry and doesn't read much. How can he help? | Listen and ask «What happens next?» | He can't help, only teachers can help with reading | He should read Ollie's book quietly to himself first | He should take a reading course before he tries
exercise: Freya reads only comics. Gran says, «That's not proper reading.» Who is right? | Freya, comics and graphic novels are real reading | Gran, only books without pictures count as reading | Gran, comics are too easy to teach any new words | Nobody, comics are not books at all
exercise: Tyler says reading is cringe but reads gaming guides for hours. What is true? | He does like reading, just about games | He hates reading, so he never reads at all | Gaming guides don't count, so he isn't reading | He is only looking at the screenshots
exercise: Ava can read alone. Why does Grandad still read aloud to her? | She hears harder books and how expression sounds | Because children who can read alone forget how | Because reading alone is bad for children's eyes | To make her stop reading by herself
exercise: Kai reads a page and puts up five fingers. What does that tell him? | The book is too hard to read alone for now | The book is just right for him | The book is far too easy for him | He must never pick that book again, not even when he is older
exercise: A Newcastle mum says her son reads under the covers, «the little bairn». What is a bairn? | A child | A little bear or another small toy animal | A torch | A secret
exercise: Nadia's friend says audiobooks are cheating. What is true? | They help with words and stories, but don't practise reading print | They are cheating and teach a child nothing at all | They are exactly the same as reading printed words, in every single way | They are only meant for grown-ups who are driving
exercise: Kids say «That book ate!» What do they mean? | It was excellent | It was eaten by the dog | It was far too long to finish | It made them hungry
exercise: Jamie says, «I'm a free reader now, I don't need the reading record.» What is true? | Free readers still need to read every day | Free readers have finished learning to read | Free readers must read only banded books | Free readers read only in class
exercise: Which reading counts as real reading? | Comics, gaming guides and novels all count | Only long novels without pictures count | Only the banded school book counts | Only books the teacher has chosen
exercise: In the five-finger test, what do you count? | Words on one page you can't read or don't know | Pages you can read all the way through in five minutes on a timer | Pictures you can see on one page | Chapters that are left
exercise: What does a book band match a book to? | How well a child can read the words alone | A child's age, whatever their reading is like | A child's favourite colour | How long the book is
exercise: Tyler, in Atlanta, says «y'all». What does it mean? | You all | Yes, always | Your aunt | You lot are wrong
test: ★★★ Kai's page: he can't read «enormous» and «gnarled», and doesn't know what «quarrel» means. He says, «Only 2 words I can't read, so 2 fingers, just right.» What's wrong? | He forgot «quarrel»: words he doesn't know count too, so it's 3 | Nothing, only words you can't say out loud count | He should count every word on the page, not just hard ones | It's 3 fingers, and 3 means far too hard for him
test: «bro audiobooks are cheating, u didn't read nothing». Fair? | No, they build words and stories, though print practice matters too | Yes, listening to a book teaches you nothing at all | Yes, only reading out loud to a teacher at school really counts as reading | No, audiobooks are better than print in every way
test: Gran: «comics rot your brain, give her a proper book.» What would you say? | Comics are real reading, and she can have other books too | Gran's right, the pictures do the work so children stop thinking for themselves | Gran's right, comics have no words worth learning | Comics are only for very little children
test: Dad: «I'm no reader, me, nowt I can do for the lad.» (nowt is northern for nothing.) What's true? | He can listen and ask questions, and that helps a lot | He's right, only good readers can help a child | He should leave reading to the school completely | He must read the whole book on his own first, before he talks to his son about it
test: ★★★ Mum signs «Read 3 pages, good» every night for a month. The teacher still doesn't know Ollie finds «ough» words hard. Why not? | The notes never say what went well or what was hard | Three pages a night is far too few to be seen | The teacher only reads the records in the summer | Ollie must have hidden the reading record
test: What would probably happen if Ollie read only books with five tricky words on every page? | He'd get stuck and stop enjoying reading | He'd learn to read twice as fast | Nothing, hard books are always best | He'd become a free reader by next week, because hard books are quicker
test: Sam: «Grandad still reads to me, I'm 9, so cringe.» Is it babyish? | No, it helps even children who can read alone | Yes, reading aloud should stop once you can read | Yes, it slows your own reading right down | No, but only if the book is a baby book
test: «lol he's a free reader so he's done with reading, innit». Right? | No, free readers still read every day | Yes, free readers have learned everything | Yes, free readers don't take books home | No, free readers go back to book bands
test: In a cartoon, a boy reads one huge book overnight and wakes up a genius. What is true in real life? | Readers grow slowly, a little every day | One hard book makes you a genius overnight | Reading at night doesn't count at all | Only reading at school makes you clever
test: ★★★ Tyler reads gaming guides, Freya reads comics and Nadia listens to audiobooks. Which one is NOT practising reading printed words? | Nadia | Tyler | Freya | None of them, all three are reading print
test: Kids swap books and talk about them. Why does that help? | It makes reading more fun and helps them choose | It gets them out of doing their homework | It means they don't need to read the books themselves, only hear about them | It only helps the teacher save money
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a book band in the primary school? | It matches early reading books to the level at which a child can read independently | It indicates that the book is intended solely for pupils of a younger age than the reader concerned | It records the number of books a child has completed in the term | It denotes the colour of the book's cover for purposes of display
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a parent who reads little may nevertheless assist a child's reading. | Listening and discussion of the story are themselves of substantial benefit | Such a parent is obliged by law to attend reading classes | The school forbids parents who read a great deal from assisting | Children learn to read exclusively by the imitation of their parents, irrespective of instruction
exam: Pursuant to the five-finger test, a pupil who encounters five unfamiliar or unreadable words upon a single page should conclude that the book is: | Presently too difficult to be read independently | Ideally suited to independent reading | Excessively easy and therefore to be discarded | To be avoided permanently thereafter
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning audiobooks. | They develop vocabulary and comprehension but do not practise the decoding of print | They constitute no form of engagement with books whatsoever | They are identical in every respect to the reading of printed text and may wholly replace it | They are permitted only for pupils who are unable to read
